  "notes": "The Bay's answer to the LA club lane, and faster: 108 full-time with a kick that lives on the offbeat 8ths and leans hard on the 3-3-2, a clap that answers the 2 and 4 from the &s at least as often as it lands on them, and cowbell and clave riding the offbeats loud enough to be part of the beat rather than under it. Hats are a continuous 16th carpet with a wide gap between accent and ghost. Harmony is two or three bright minor triads and nothing more, and the 808 is punchy and sits on the root — the energy here is rhythmic, not melodic. Fills stay on the snare and the percs, because the 16th clap roll belongs to Mustard. Confidence is medium: the compendium documents the West Coast club grammar this is measured against, and the Bay-specific numbers are extrapolated from it plus public genre references.",
